  <!-- ── FAQ ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions — Chicago / Midwest Applicants</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Which states use the Chicago consulate for the Spain NLV?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The Consulate General of Spain in Chicago covers: Illinois, Wisconsin, Minnesota, Iowa, Missouri, Indiana, Ohio, Michigan, and Kentucky. If you are a legal resident of any of these states, you must apply at the Chicago consulate — regardless of which consulate may be geographically closer or have shorter wait times. You cannot choose a different consulate.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is the Chicago consulate appointment wait time long?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Generally, the Chicago consulate has more manageable appointment availability than the highest-volume consulates (New York, Los Angeles). Most applicants secure appointments 4–6 weeks out. That said, demand increases in spring (March–May) as people plan moves for summer. Checking the appointment portal regularly — and early in the morning — gives you the best chance of finding a slot quickly. Start your document preparation before you have a confirmed appointment date.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I use my Illinois or Midwest pension to qualify?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Public sector pensions from the Midwest — Illinois TRS, Illinois SERS, Wisconsin WRS, Ohio PERS, Ohio STRS, Michigan MPSERS, or union pensions from the UAW, Teamsters, and similar organizations — all qualify as passive income for the NLV. You'll need the pension award letter and 3–6 months of bank statements confirming regular monthly deposits.</p>
            <p>Combined with Social Security, most Midwest public-sector retirees easily meet or exceed the income threshold.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do Ohio or Michigan residents have to travel to Chicago?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Ohio and Michigan fall under the jurisdiction of the Chicago consulate. Residents of Columbus, Cleveland, Cincinnati, Detroit, Grand Rapids, and other cities in these states must travel to Chicago, IL for their in-person NLV appointment. There are no Spanish consulates in Ohio or Michigan. Plan your trip to Chicago accordingly — many applicants combine the consulate visit with other Chicago activities to make the journey worthwhile.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How does a remote worker or early retiree qualify for the NLV from Illinois?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The NLV requires passive, non-active income — meaning income you receive without actively working. Investment portfolios (dividends, interest), rental income from US properties, business sale proceeds held in interest-bearing accounts, and similar sources can qualify. However, if you are still actively employed or doing consulting work, those income streams do not qualify for the NLV's passive income requirement. If you're planning to transition to passive income ahead of your application, a specialist consultation is strongly recommended to time the application correctly.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can a couple apply together at the Chicago consulate?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Couples apply together at the same appointment if both partners live within the Chicago consulate's jurisdiction. Each person must submit their own complete document set — individual FBI checks, individual medical certificates, individual visa application forms (EX-01). Financial documentation can be shared or combined to demonstrate household income. The combined income threshold for a couple in 2026 is approximately $4,000/month. If income is split across two individuals, both sets of statements are submitted together.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

